1
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:09,786
Photosynthesis: six carbon dioxide plus twelve water give glucose, six oxygen, and six water.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:11,330
Why twelve water?

3
00:00:11,330 --> 00:00:14,325
Because all the oxygen released comes from water.

4
00:00:14,325 --> 00:00:17,846
Heavy-oxygen experiments in nineteen forty-one proved it.

5
00:00:19,067 --> 00:00:21,152
It happens in the chloroplast.

6
00:00:21,152 --> 00:00:25,671
Two membranes enclose the stroma, a fluid full of enzymes.

7
00:00:25,900 --> 00:00:29,157
Inside are thylakoids, stacked into grana.

8
00:00:29,157 --> 00:00:31,198
Their inner space is the lumen.

9
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:33,810
Light reaction: thylakoids.

10
00:00:33,810 --> 00:00:35,633
Dark reaction: stroma.

11
00:00:37,234 --> 00:00:40,322
Chlorophyll a is the reaction-centre pigment.

12
00:00:40,322 --> 00:00:45,996
Chlorophyll b and carotenoids are accessory pigments that pass energy to it.

13
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:49,848
Chlorophyll absorbs blue and red light and reflects green.

14
00:00:51,067 --> 00:00:53,386
The light reaction is the Z-scheme.

15
00:00:53,386 --> 00:00:55,431
Height shows electron energy.

16
00:00:55,431 --> 00:01:02,612
Photosystem two, P680, and photosystem one, P700, work in series.

17
00:01:02,834 --> 00:01:07,789
Light excites P680, and an electron jumps to pheophytin.

18
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:16,764
It runs downhill through plastoquinone, cytochrome b6 f, and plastocyanin, pumping protons into the lumen.

19
00:01:16,967 --> 00:01:19,247
Light excites P700.

20
00:01:19,247 --> 00:01:26,472
The electron jumps again, then through ferredoxin to F N R, which makes NADPH.

21
00:01:26,700 --> 00:01:30,959
P680 replaces its lost electron by splitting water.

22
00:01:30,959 --> 00:01:35,129
Photolysis releases oxygen and protons into the lumen.

23
00:01:35,334 --> 00:01:41,924
This one-way flow, from water to NADP plus, is non-cyclic photophosphorylation.

24
00:01:41,924 --> 00:01:45,615
It makes ATP, NADPH and oxygen.

25
00:01:45,834 --> 00:01:49,794
In cyclic flow, photosystem one works alone.

26
00:01:49,794 --> 00:01:53,166
Electrons loop back, making only ATP.

27
00:01:53,166 --> 00:01:54,937
No NADPH, no oxygen.

28
00:01:56,567 --> 00:02:00,167
In the thylakoid, protons build up inside the lumen.

29
00:02:01,300 --> 00:02:06,692
They flow back through ATP synthase, which spins and makes ATP.

30
00:02:06,692 --> 00:02:08,233
This is chemiosmosis.

31
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:14,420
Oxygen is released in the lumen; ATP and NADPH form in the stroma.

32
00:02:16,100 --> 00:02:23,823
Non-cyclic uses both photosystems, splits water, and makes ATP, NADPH and oxygen.

33
00:02:23,823 --> 00:02:28,407
Cyclic uses photosystem one only and makes ATP alone.

34
00:02:28,634 --> 00:02:29,560
Why both?

35
00:02:29,560 --> 00:02:35,081
The Calvin cycle needs ATP to NADPH at three to two.

36
00:02:35,081 --> 00:02:39,227
Non-cyclic gives less ATP, so cyclic tops it up.

37
00:02:41,634 --> 00:02:42,886
Pause and think.

38
00:02:42,886 --> 00:02:45,516
What does cyclic photophosphorylation produce?

39
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:53,678
ATP only.

40
00:02:53,678 --> 00:02:59,303
No water is split, so no oxygen; no NADPH is made.

41
00:02:59,303 --> 00:03:01,759
ATP and oxygen is the trap.

42
00:03:02,534 --> 00:03:08,488
The Calvin cycle runs in the stroma, powered by ATP and NADPH.

43
00:03:08,700 --> 00:03:17,583
Three RuBP, fifteen carbons, plus three carbon dioxide make six 3-PGA, eighteen carbons, via RuBisCO.

44
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:22,710
3-PGA is the first stable product: these are C3 plants.

45
00:03:22,934 --> 00:03:27,803
Six ATP and six NADPH reduce it to six G3P.

46
00:03:28,034 --> 00:03:31,626
One G3P leaves the cycle to make sugar.

47
00:03:31,834 --> 00:03:37,705
Five G3P regenerate three RuBP, using three ATP.

48
00:03:37,934 --> 00:03:43,228
So one G3P costs nine ATP and six NADPH.

49
00:03:45,834 --> 00:03:50,376
Per carbon dioxide: three ATP and two NADPH.

50
00:03:50,600 --> 00:03:52,471
Glucose needs six turns.

51
00:03:52,700 --> 00:03:55,941
Eighteen ATP and twelve NADPH.

52
00:03:56,167 --> 00:03:57,816
Ratio: three to two.

53
00:04:00,334 --> 00:04:06,345
RuBisCO can also grab oxygen, since air has far more oxygen than carbon dioxide.

54
00:04:06,567 --> 00:04:12,981
Then it makes a useless two-carbon compound, salvaged at a loss of carbon: photorespiration.

55
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:16,709
It worsens on hot, dry days when stomata close.

56
00:04:18,334 --> 00:04:27,671
C4 plants like maize and sugarcane avoid it with Kranz anatomy: bundle-sheath cells ring each vein, surrounded by mesophyll.

57
00:04:27,900 --> 00:04:35,082
In the mesophyll, PEP carboxylase fixes carbon dioxide into oxaloacetate, a four-carbon acid.

58
00:04:35,300 --> 00:04:41,257
This becomes malate, which moves into the bundle sheath and releases carbon dioxide around RuBisCO.

59
00:04:41,467 --> 00:04:49,148
Pyruvate returns and is recycled to PEP, costing extra ATP: thirty per glucose instead of eighteen.

60
00:04:49,367 --> 00:04:51,699
C4 separates the steps in space.

61
00:04:54,500 --> 00:04:55,739
Pause and think.

62
00:04:55,739 --> 00:04:59,475
What is the first stable product of C4 photosynthesis?

63
00:05:06,367 --> 00:05:09,129
Oxaloacetic acid, four carbons.

64
00:05:09,129 --> 00:05:13,061
Malic acid is the trap: it forms one step later.

65
00:05:13,061 --> 00:05:18,045
PEP is the acceptor, and 3-PGA is the C3 product.

66
00:05:18,834 --> 00:05:22,740
CAM plants like pineapple and aloe separate the steps in time.

67
00:05:22,967 --> 00:05:27,810
At night, stomata open and carbon dioxide is stored as malic acid.

68
00:05:28,034 --> 00:05:33,481
By day, stomata close and the stored carbon dioxide enters the Calvin cycle.

69
00:05:35,100 --> 00:05:38,682
C3 plants make 3-PGA first.

70
00:05:38,682 --> 00:05:45,284
C4 and CAM make oxaloacetate first, separating the steps in space or time.

71
00:05:46,500 --> 00:05:49,642
Blackman's law says the slowest factor limits the rate.

72
00:05:49,867 --> 00:05:53,137
In dim light, light limits photosynthesis.

73
00:05:53,137 --> 00:05:56,328
Where net gain is zero is the light compensation point.

74
00:05:56,534 --> 00:05:59,530
In bright light, carbon dioxide limits it.

75
00:05:59,530 --> 00:06:02,104
More carbon dioxide raises the plateau.

76
00:06:03,734 --> 00:06:07,512
Example: rice at twenty degrees in bright light.

77
00:06:07,512 --> 00:06:12,447
Doubling light does nothing; doubling carbon dioxide doubles the rate.

78
00:06:12,447 --> 00:06:14,494
What limits it, and what if we warm it?

79
00:06:14,700 --> 00:06:21,493
Doubling the light changes nothing, so light is not limiting: the plant is already light-saturated.

80
00:06:21,700 --> 00:06:27,345
Raising carbon dioxide doubles the rate, so carbon dioxide was the limiting factor.

81
00:06:27,567 --> 00:06:33,542
At eight hundred parts per million carbon dioxide is plentiful, so the next factor in line takes over.

82
00:06:33,767 --> 00:06:42,114
Twenty degrees is below the optimum for rice's enzymes, so temperature now limits, and warming to thirty raises the rate further.

83
00:06:48,100 --> 00:06:48,333
Respiration is the stepwise oxidation of food to release usable energy.

